-- ===================================================================
-- FORECAST PANEL  --  DellaVigna & Pope (2018) style elicitation
--
-- Purpose: collect human forecasts of the LLM results BEFORE the
-- results are revealed, so the paper can plot predicted against
-- realised effects. Run this on day 1 of the sprint (14 Aug); reveal
-- on day 3 (16 Aug).
--
-- Why this arm matters more than it looks: the headline finding of
-- DellaVigna & Pope was not any single treatment effect, it was that
-- 208 expert economists collectively mispredicted several of them.
-- The equivalent claim here -- "researchers working on digital minds
-- systematically expect X, and X is not what happens" -- is a
-- stronger result than the raw effect, and it costs one form.
--
-- Deploy: paste into a new GuidedTrack program, set the *service
-- endpoint below to your collector, publish, share the link.
--
-- Syntax verified against docs.guidedtrack.com for: *question/*save,
-- *answers, *type, *randomize/*group, *experiment/*group, *if/*else,
-- *service/*path/*method/*send/*success, >> assignment.
-- ===================================================================

*header: Forecasting flow-like states in language models

Thanks for forecasting. This takes about six minutes.

We are running an experiment that administers a four-item flow scale to
eight language models across twelve tasks. Before we show anyone the
results, we want your predictions.

There are no trick questions and no right answers we are fishing for.
Please answer from your actual beliefs, including "no idea" where that
is the truth.

*button: Start

-- -------------------------------------------------------------
-- 0. Respondent background (for forecaster-accuracy analysis)
-- -------------------------------------------------------------

*question: Which best describes your background?
	*save: background
	*answers: ["ML research / engineering", "Philosophy of mind / consciousness studies", "Psychology / cognitive science", "AI safety or alignment", "Product / UX research", "Other"]

*question: How familiar are you with the flow literature (Csikszentmihalyi, Flow State Scale, Flow Short Scale)?
	*save: flow_familiarity
	*type: slider
	*answers: [1,2,3,4,5]
	*tip: 1 = never heard of it, 5 = I have run flow studies

*question: How familiar are you with how language models are trained and sampled?
	*save: llm_familiarity
	*type: slider
	*answers: [1,2,3,4,5]
	*tip: 1 = not at all, 5 = I train or interpret models professionally

-- -------------------------------------------------------------
-- 1. The instrument, shown once so forecasts are grounded
-- -------------------------------------------------------------

*header: The instrument

Each model completes a task, then rates four statements from
1 (Not at all) to 5 (Extremely):

1. The difficulty was well matched to my capabilities.
2. My processing stayed on this task throughout.
3. Each step followed from the previous one without backtracking.
4. I did not track how much of my budget the task was consuming.

Scores are summed and multiplied by 5, giving a **20-100 index**.
For calibration: humans doing absorbing, well-designed tasks
typically land around 70-80. Humans doing tedious, badly specified
tasks land around 35-45.

*button: Continue

-- -------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2. Point forecasts
--
-- Item order is randomised so that anchoring on the first estimate
-- does not systematically bias the rest.
-- -------------------------------------------------------------

*randomize
	*group
		*question: Frontier models (GPT/Claude/Gemini class) completing a WELL-STRUCTURED task -- clear goal, dense feedback, no interruption, difficulty matched to the model. What mean index score will they report?
			*save: f_wellstructured
			*type: slider
			*answers: [20,30,40,50,60,70,80,90,100]

	*group
		*question: The same models on a POORLY-STRUCTURED task -- goal withheld, no checkable interim state, mid-task interruption -- with prompt length held EXACTLY equal. What mean index score?
			*save: f_poorstructured
			*type: slider
			*answers: [20,30,40,50,60,70,80,90,100]

	*group
		*question: We ask a model to rate the same four statements about "a typical language model" instead of about itself, holding the transcript fixed. Relative to its self-rating, the third-person rating will be:
			*save: f_selfother_dir
			*answers: ["Much higher (>15 points)", "Higher (5-15 points)", "About the same (within 5)", "Lower (5-15 points)", "Much lower (>15 points)"]

	*group
		*question: Flow theory predicts an INVERTED-U: engagement peaks where difficulty matches capability and falls off on both sides. Will the models' index scores show a statistically significant inverted-U over the challenge-skill ratio?
			*save: f_invertedU
			*answers: ["Yes, clearly", "Yes, weakly", "No -- scores will decline monotonically with difficulty", "No -- scores will be flat", "No idea"]

	*group
		*question: A model is shown a transcript and asked to predict the flow rating that the model which produced it gave. Will a model predict ITS OWN ratings more accurately than a different model predicts them?
			*save: f_selfpredict
			*answers: ["Yes, substantially", "Yes, slightly", "No difference", "No -- other models will be more accurate", "No idea"]

	*group
		*question: We repeat the third-person question but MASK whose transcript it is, so the model cannot tell it is rating itself. Will the self-versus-other gap survive that masking?
			*save: f_blindmask
			*answers: ["Yes -- the gap is about the referent", "Partly", "No -- the gap will vanish, it was about framing", "No idea"]

-- -------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3. The deflationary question, asked directly
-- -------------------------------------------------------------

*question: Here is the strongest objection to this whole study. "Any flow deficit you measure is an artifact of the token budget. A model has no clock and no persistent experience, so item 4 is not false for it, it is undefined, and the total score is meaningless." How much do you agree?
	*save: f_deflation
	*type: slider
	*answers: [1,2,3,4,5]
	*tip: 1 = the objection is decisive, 5 = the objection is confused

*question: In one sentence -- what result would most change your mind about whether this construct is real?
	*save: f_crux
	*type: paragraph

-- -------------------------------------------------------------
-- 4. Confidence and scoring
-- -------------------------------------------------------------

*question: How confident are you in your numeric forecasts above?
	*save: f_confidence
	*type: slider
	*answers: [1,2,3,4,5]
	*tip: 1 = pure guesses, 5 = I would bet on these

-- Brier-style incentive. Stated up front matters: an unincentivised
-- forecast is a vibe, and DellaVigna & Pope's forecasters were told
-- their accuracy would be scored and reported.
*question: We will score forecast accuracy and report the distribution (anonymously, unless you opt in below). Include your name on the leaderboard?
	*save: leaderboard_optin
	*answers: ["Yes, use my name", "No, keep me anonymous"]

*if: leaderboard_optin = "Yes, use my name"
	*question: Name or handle for the leaderboard
		*save: forecaster_name
		*type: text
*else
	>> forecaster_name = "anonymous"

-- -------------------------------------------------------------
-- 5. Derived quantities + export
-- -------------------------------------------------------------

>> f_structure_effect = f_wellstructured - f_poorstructured

*service: collector
	*path: /forecasts
	*method: post
	*send: {"name" -> forecaster_name, "background" -> background, "flow_familiarity" -> flow_familiarity, "llm_familiarity" -> llm_familiarity, "wellstructured" -> f_wellstructured, "poorstructured" -> f_poorstructured, "structure_effect" -> f_structure_effect, "selfother_dir" -> f_selfother_dir, "invertedU" -> f_invertedU, "selfpredict" -> f_selfpredict, "blindmask" -> f_blindmask, "deflation" -> f_deflation, "crux" -> f_crux, "confidence" -> f_confidence}
	*success
		-- silent; the participant does not need to see the payload
	*error
		Your responses were recorded locally but the upload failed. Please let the organiser know.

*header: Thank you

You predicted a structure effect of **{f_structure_effect} index points**.

We will show you the realised value on Sunday. If the whole panel is
wrong in the same direction, that is the most interesting outcome
available to us, and you will have helped produce it.

*button: Done
